ires to or is nominated or qualified for an office, membership, or award\nL49: \nL50: a candidate for governor\nL51: \nL52: a candidate for \"Manager of the Year\"\nL53: \nL54: the best candidate for the job\nL55: \nL56: b\nL57: \nL58: : one likely or suited to undergo or be chosen for something specified\nL59: \nL60: a candidate for surgery\nL61: \nL62: 2\nL63: : a student in the process of meeting final requirements for a degree\nL64: \nL65: a PhD candidate\nL66: \nL67: 【18†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】\nL68: ## Did you know?\nL69: \nL70: Roman Fashion Is Responsible for Candidate\nL71: When a man running for public office in ancient Rome greeted voters in the 【19†Forum】, the center of judicial and public business, he wore a toga that had been whitened with chalk. As a result, the Latin word for someone seeking office came to be candidatus, meaning literally \"clothed in white.\" Candidatus, in turn, comes from the adjective candidus, meaning \"white.\" Candidatus was adopted into English as candidate, and since the 17th century that word has had an uncontested seat in the language.\nL72: ## Synonyms of candidate\nL73: \nL74: Relevance\nL75: \nL76:   * 【20†applicant】\nL77:   * 【21†contender】\nL78: \nL79: 【17†See All Synonyms & Antonyms in Thesaurus】\nL80: ## Examples of candidate in a Sentence\nL81: \nL82: Candidates can apply in person or send a resume. a Ph.D. candidate in linguistics The program has several doctoral candidates.\nL83: \nL84: Recent Examples on the Web\nL85: \nL86: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【22†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL87: Both candidates are spending the final week before Election Day barnstorming the state. — Ben Paviour, Sacbee.com, 28 Oct. 2026 Sanders, the Vermont socialist and two-time presidential candidate, is likely to chair the Senate panel responsible for overseeing labor, education, and healthcare next year if Democrats reclaim the chamber. — David Sivak, The Washington Examiner, 28 Oct. 2026 No major candidate advocated a return to the long-moribund peace process. — Giovanna Dell'orto, Chicago Tribune, 28 Oct.\nL88: 2026 With a major election less than a week away, candidates and their consultants are pounding Floridians with a barrage of words — some intended to cast themselves in a flattering light, some intended to smear and degrade their opponents. — Orlando Sentinel Editorial Board, The Orlando Sentinel, 28 Oct. 2026 【23†See All Example Sentences for candidate】\nL89: ## Word History\nL90: \nL91: Etymology\nL92: \nL93: borrowed from Latin candidātus \"man running for public office,\" noun derivative from candidātus \"dressed in white clothes,\" from candidus \"bright, white\" + -ātus 【24†-ate entry 3】; from the whitened toga worn by candidates for office in ancient Rome — more at 【25†candid entry 1】\nL94: \nL95: First Known Use\nL96: \nL97: 1600, in the meaning defined at 【26†sense 1a】\nL98: \nL99: Time Traveler\nL100: \nL101: The first known use of candidate was in 1600\nL102: \nL103: 【27†See more words from the same year】\nL104: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL105: \nL106: 【28†candidacy】\nL107: \nL108: candidate\nL109: \nL110: 【29†candidature】\nL111: \nL112: 【30†See all Nearby Words】\nL113: \nL114: ## Articles Related to candidate\nL115: \nL116: 【31†More Commonly Mispronounced Words We're here to help】 【32†12 Political Putdowns For When 'Lowdown Crook' Isn't Specific Enough】 【33†17 Red, White, and Blue Political Terms Red tape, blue laws, and more】\nL117: ## Cite this Entry\nL118: \nL119: Style\nL120: \nL121: [Select]\nL122: \nL123: “Candidate.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/candidate. Accessed 2 Nov. 2026.\nL124: \nL125: 【34†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL126: ## Kids Definition\nL127: \nL128: candidate\nL129: \nL130: noun\nL131: \nL132: can·​di·​date 【16†ˈkan-də-ˌdāt 】\nL133: \nL134: ˈkan-ə-,\nL135: \nL136: -dət\nL137: \nL138: : one who runs in an election contest or is proposed for an office or honor\nL139: \nL140: Etymology\nL141: \nL142: from Latin candidatus \"candidate,\" from candidatus (adjective) \"dressed in white,\" from candidus \"shining white,\" from candēre \"to be bright, shine\" — related to 【35†candid】, 【36†candle】\nL143: \nL144: Word Origin\nL145: In ancient Rome it was the custom for a person who wanted to be elected to public office to wear a toga that had been rubbed with chalk to make it white. The Latin word for \"dressed in white\" was candidatus. In time this word came to be used for the person himself, or the candidate.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConstituencies (Constituents) - UK Parliament (https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/constituencies/)\nciteturn36view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/constituencies/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 233\nL0: 【0††www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Skip to main content】 【2†Menu】\nL3: \nL4: Menu\nL5: \nL6: Select an area to explore\nL7: \nL8:   * 【2†Business】\nL9:   * 【2†MPs, Lords & offices】\nL10:   * 【2†About】\nL11:   * 【2†Get involved】\nL12:   * 【2†Visit】\nL13:   * 【2†Learning】\nL14:   * 【3†Shop†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL15:   * 【2†More】\nL16: \nL17:   * Or type a keyword to search\nL18: \nL19: [Input: Search UK Parliament]\nL20: \nL21: [Button: Search]\nL22: \nL23: 【2†Close】\nL24: \nL25: [Input]\nL26: \nL27: 【2†Close】\nL28: ## Parliamentary business\nL29: \nL30: Find out what’s on today at the House of Commons and House of Lords. Track current bills, keep up with committees, watch live footage and follow topical issues.\nL31: \nL32: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL33: ### In this section\nL34: \nL35:   * 【4†Parliamentary business home】\nL36:   * 【5†House of Commons】\nL37: